            <ArticleTitle>Co-morbidity in patients with sight-threatening diabetic retinopathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>719-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the level of co-morbidity in patients with sight-threatening diabetic retinopathy in the United Kingdom.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Questionnaires were completed by patients undergoing first photocoagulation treatment for diabetic maculopathy or proliferative retinopathy during a 2 month period throughout the UK.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Overall 15% of patients described angina, 9% had suffered a myocardial infarction and 6% a stroke. Self-reported renal disease was present in 8.5%. Foot ulceration was described by 10% of patients, and 4% had undergone an amputation. 35.5% of patients were on treatment for hypertension. 17.5% of patients had been hospitalised in the previous 6 months, and 3% of patients had died within 9 months of the laser treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There was significant co-morbidity in these patients, which may affect the management of their retinopathy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
